---
// guides/how-domain-registration-works.astro
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How Domain Registration Works — TLD Directory"
  description="The lifecycle of a domain registration: from availability check to renewal, and who does what at each step."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
    <Breadcrumb items={[
      { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
      { label: 'How Registration Works' }
    ]} />

    <article class="prose prose-ink max-w-none">
      <header class="mb-8">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink-900">How Domain Registration Works</h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink-600 text-lg">The complete lifecycle — availability, registration, management, renewal, and expiry.</p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>The players</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>You (the registrant)</strong> — the person or organisation that holds the registration.</li>
          <li><strong>Registrar</strong> — the retailer you buy from (e.g. Cloudflare, Namecheap, Porkbun).</li>
          <li><strong>Registry</strong> — the operator of the TLD (e.g. VeriSign for <code>.com</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>ICANN/IANA</strong> — oversight and root zone coordination (gTLDs).</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The lifecycle</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Availability check</strong> — You search a registrar. They query the registry's database (via EPP) or a cached copy. If the domain is free, you can proceed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Order & payment</strong> — You provide contact details (registrant, admin, tech, billing) and pay. The registrar sends a <code>create</code> command to the registry via EPP (Extensible Provisioning Protocol).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Registry creates the domain</strong> — The registry adds the domain to its authoritative database, assigns name servers (or parks it), and returns success.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Propagation</strong> — The registry publishes the new delegation to its authoritative name servers. This is near-instant for most TLDs (seconds to minutes).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Active registration</strong> — You can now set DNS records, use email, build a site. The domain is yours for the registration period (1–10 years).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Renewal</strong> — Before expiry, you pay the registrar. They send a <code>renew</code> command to the registry. The domain stays active.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Expiry & grace periods</strong> — If not renewed:
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Auto-renew grace period</strong> (0–45 days): registrar may auto-renew on your behalf; you can still renew normally.</li>
              <li><strong>Redemption grace period</strong> (30 days): domain is deleted from the zone; recovery is possible but expensive (registry fee + registrar fee).</li>
              <li><strong>Pending delete</strong> (5 days): domain cannot be recovered; soon drops to available.</li>
              <li><strong>Available</strong> — anyone can register it.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Key concepts</h2>
        <dl>
          <dt><strong>EPP (Extensible Provisioning Protocol)</strong></dt>
          <dd>Standard XML protocol registrars use to talk to registries (create, renew, transfer, update).</dd>

          <dt><strong>Registrant contact</strong></dt>
          <dd>The legal owner. Keep this accurate — it's used for verification, disputes, and recovery.</dd>

          <dt><strong>Name servers</strong></dt>
          <dd>Where DNS queries go. You can use the registrar's DNS, your hosting provider's, or a third party (Cloudflare, AWS Route 53, etc.).</dd>

          <dt><strong>Auth code (EPP code)</strong></dt>
          <dd>Transfer password. Required to move a domain to another registrar. Keep it secret.</dd>

          <dt><strong>Domain lock</strong></dt>
          <dd>Prevents unauthorised transfers. Enable it unless you're actively transferring.</dd>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Transfers</h2>
        <p>Moving a domain between registrars (gTLDs):</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Unlock the domain at the current registrar.</li>
          <li>Get the auth code.</li>
          <li>Initiate transfer at the new registrar (pay for 1 year renewal).</li>
          <li>Confirm via email (FOA — Form of Authorization).</li>
          <li>Wait 5 days (or less if current registrar approves).</li>
        </ol>
        <p>ccTLD transfers vary — some use the same process, others require paperwork or don't support transfers at all.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Common pitfalls</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Letting a domain expire</strong> — recovery is expensive; the domain may be snapped up by drop-catchers.</li>
          <li><strong>Using a personal email for registrant contact</strong> — if you lose access, you can't prove ownership.</li>
          <li><strong>Not enabling auto-renew</strong> — the #1 cause of accidental loss.</li>
          <li><strong>Ignoring registrar emails</strong> — ICANN requires annual WHOIS accuracy verification.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <footer class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-ink-200">
        <nav class="flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related guides">
          <a href="/guides/registry-vs-registrar/" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 font-medium">← Registry vs Registrar</a>
          <a href="/guides/registration-vs-renewal/" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 font-medium">Registration vs Renewal →</a>
        </nav>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>